<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kisangani</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Stanleyville</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kisangani is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-river">Congo River</a> in the north-east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a>, at the head of navigation below the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boyoma-falls">Boyoma Falls</a>. Founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-morton-stanley">Henry Morton Stanley</a> in 1883 as Stanley Falls station and later called Stanleyville, it was a centre of the Simba rebellion in 1964, and in 1999–2000 Ugandan and Rwandan armies fought each other for control of the city during the Second Congo War.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kisangani.jpg" alt="Kisangani" width="320" height="229" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yannick Mbangi · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vi_lle.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
